## Steps

### Step 1: Mount and configure each backend

Each backend starts with a `secrets enable` call and a connection or role configuration. The mount path is the address every later command uses.

```bash
# KV v2 — static storage
vault secrets enable -path=secret kv -version=2

# Database — dynamic credentials
vault secrets enable -path=db database
vault write db/config/postgres \
  plugin_name=postgresql-database-plugin \
  connection_url="postgres://{{username}}:{{password}}@localhost:5432/admin?sslmode=disable"
vault write db/roles/app \
  db_name=postgres \
  default_ttl=1h \
  max_ttl=24h

# Cloud (AWS) — dynamic IAM credentials
vault secrets enable -path=aws aws
vault write aws/config/root \
  access_key=AKIA... \
  secret_key=wJalr...
vault write aws/roles/ci-role \
  credential_type=iam_user \
  default_sts_ttl=1h \
  max_sts_ttl=24h
```

The KV mount takes no connection details because it stores whatever the operator writes. The database and cloud mounts each need a privileged connection or role that Vault uses to generate credentials.

