        "returns": "the stored value, unchanged",
        "lease": "none",
    },
    "database": {
        "write": "vault write db/roles/<name> ... (role config only)",
        "read": "vault read db/creds/<role>",
        "returns": "a generated username/password pair",
        "lease": "lease_id with a TTL",
    },
    "cloud": {
        "write": "vault write aws/roles/<name> ... (role config only)",
        "read": "vault read aws/creds/<role>",
        "returns": "a generated access key/secret key pair",
        "lease": "lease_id with a TTL",
    },
}
for name, info in READ_WRITE.items():
    print(f"{name:<10}: read={info['read']:<34} lease={info['lease']}")
</code_cell>

### Step 3: Compare lifecycle and revocation

The load-bearing difference between backends is what happens after a credential is issued. KV credentials never rotate and must be deleted by hand. Database and cloud credentials rotate on a TTL and can be revoked immediately by lease ID.

```bash
# KV — no rotation, explicit cleanup
vault kv delete secret/cloud-iam/static

# Database / cloud — revoke by lease ID
vault lease revoke <lease_id>
```

Revoking a database or cloud lease immediately invalidates the credential and, for database, drops the generated user. Revoking a KV path only removes the stored value; any client that already read it still holds the secret.

<code_cell>
